WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.537 --> 00:00:03.120
PRESENTER: Hello, and welcome
to addressing racial disparities

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.200
in health care.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.780
Thanks to all of our participants
who are joining us today

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.590
from all around the world.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:11.820
This webinar is brought to you by
Harvard Online and the Disparity

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.350
Solutions Center at
Massachusetts General Hospital.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:17.780
We're excited to introduce
you to our moderator

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.400
and panelists for today's event.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.510
Our moderator is Aswita
Tan-McGrory, one of the faculty

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.780
who teaches our upcoming course,
"Reducing Racial Disparities in Health

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:28.710
Care."

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.890
She is also the Director of the
Disparities Solutions Center

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:36.030
and Director of Equity and Care
Implementation at Massachusetts General

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:36.600
Hospital.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.230
Hello, Aswita.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:38.393
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:38.393 --> 00:00:40.810
And now, I'll let you introduce
the rest of our panelists.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.268
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Thank you, Jim.

00:00:42.268 --> 00:00:46.560
I want to welcome everybody and
thank them for joining us today.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.960
And I have three colleagues,
and also friends, with me today.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.970
I have Dr. Lenny Lopez, Andrea
Madu, and Dr. Esteban Barreto.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.890
And so I'm going to
kick off this webinar.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.710
We're going to have a lively discussion.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.959
And as was previously mentioned,
if you have questions,

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:07.890
please submit them
through the Q&amp;A function,

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:12.040
and we will use the last 15
minutes to go through those.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.270
But first, I want to ask
each of the panelists

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.990
to go ahead and introduce
themselves, and then really

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:25.290
kind of let us know what is sort of
the driving force for you in doing

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.620
this work around addressing
racial and ethnic disparities

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.600
and working on equity.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.768
And Lenny, I'll start with you, and
then Andrea, I'll have you go second.

00:01:33.768 --> 00:01:35.310
LENNY LOPEZ: Well, thank you, Aswita.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:40.420
It's wonderful to be here and on this
panel on this very important topic.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:44.700
My name is Lenny Lopez, and
I am a Professor of Medicine

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.630
and the Chief of Hospital Medicine
at the University of California San

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.140
Francisco and the VA
Medical Center here.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:57.570
And I'm also a health-care disparities
researcher and implementation science

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:58.590
researcher.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:04.890
I think for me, the disparities work and
illumination of disparities and really

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:08.550
bringing equity to health care
comes from my family experiences,

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:09.960
and also by patient care.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.840
Every day, I see individuals
that are challenged

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:19.980
by access barriers, challenges around
language barriers, lack of access

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.510
to high-quality care.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.170
And most importantly, from the moment
that they enter the health care system,

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.750
from registration all the
way to their outcomes,

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.980
equity has to be the
number-one True North.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.998
So that's really-- my passion
and energy comes from that.

00:02:34.998 --> 00:02:36.540
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Thank you, Lenny.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.500
Andrea?

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.650
ANDREA MADU: Hi, everyone.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:39.870
My name is Andrea Madu.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:44.040
So happy to be here and excited to
have a conversation on this topic.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.440
I am currently a consultant
at the Bridgespan Group,

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.170
and I work largely with
nonprofits and philanthropists

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:53.250
to accelerate their impact and solve
really tough challenges centered

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.150
around equity.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:57.420
But I started my career in health
care, working to address disparities

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.780
for youth regarding HIV/AIDS,
and then working at the DSC

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.030
with the panelists on this call.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.490
I'm really interested in
this work, because I've

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.550
seen for myself the differential
treatment that people

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:11.460
receive based on language proficiency.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.580
My family emigrated from Nigeria
in the '80s, and I remember going

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.880
to the hospital as a child and seeing
how doctors treated my grandmother,

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.380
who could not speak English--

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.150
very different from me and my siblings.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.720
And also my mom, who speaks English
fluently, but has a strong accent,

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.750
would have to use her knowledge as a
nurse to really navigate the health

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:31.230
care system and for her to get respect
and be treated with the respect

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.460
that she deserved.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.680
So those experiences
have always fueled me

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.990
to work on reducing health
disparities in health care,

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:40.380
and I've seen how this poor treatment
can translate to poor outcomes

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:41.740
for patients.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.960
And then, personally, for myself, I know
there's a very high maternal mortality

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.970
rate for Black women
regardless of class.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.972
And I've always been terrified of
the prospection of giving birth,

00:03:50.972 --> 00:03:53.680
because I know what the outcomes
are for people who look like me.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.920
And so I'm always terrified
for my family members,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.180
even though they're in
the medical profession.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.530
So in a way, it affects me, it
affects the people in my family,

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.558
and that realization has
made me want to advocate

00:04:04.558 --> 00:04:07.350
for people who may not have the
privilege and knowledge to navigate

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:08.740
through that health care system.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.323
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Thank you, Andrea.

00:04:10.323 --> 00:04:10.860
Esteban.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.660
ESTEBAN BARRETO: Thank you, Aswita.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:13.840
Great to be here.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.960
Thank you so much for the invitation.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.640
Similarly to Lenny and Andrea, a
lot of my passion for this work

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.010
comes from lived experience.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:26.640
As an immigrant to the
US myself, I also had

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.250
to navigate different systems with my
family, especially the medical system.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:40.590
And having family members go through
different language barriers really

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:45.250
allowed me to focus
on this type of work.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.230
I would also say, from
early in college, I

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:52.230
started working as a
research assistant and worked

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:55.710
in a variety of different research
and quality-improvement projects.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:05:02.280
And that gave me a great exposure into
inclusive data-collection techniques,

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.130
also different sampling
techniques that we

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:09.900
could use that allows us to hear
from those patients who may otherwise

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.140
be excluded from routine assessments.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.840
So I will say it's a combination
of both lived experience,

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.140
and also early professional
experience, where it really

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:24.990
allowed me to explore barriers
in a more tailored way

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.500
and hear from those
voices who may, as I said,

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.820
may otherwise be excluded
from different assessments.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.980
Once again, great to be here and
on this important conversation.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.230
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Thank you.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:41.070
I think the overwhelming theme I'm
hearing is, this is personal to us,

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:41.940
right?

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:42.810
Very personal.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:44.910
I mean, not only do we--

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:49.350
are we motivated because, as many
people say, it's the right thing to do,

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:52.440
but also it resonates with many of us.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.400
It's the people that we love,
that we see as patients.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:02.760
It's the experiences we
had in our past childhood.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:06.970
So I think there's a lot
here when we do this work.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:11.460
And I think it's also a way that we
have to sort of protect ourselves.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:12.780
I'll just put that out there.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.850
This is tough work.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:18.990
And having a community of like-minded
colleagues and friends like you guys

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:24.960
goes a long way into creating
that sort of safe space.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.170
A lot has changed in this
field since the pandemic.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.920
And I kind of want to talk
about that, because we were

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.890
doing this work before the pandemic--

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:41.070
really launched the center with
the IOM report on equal treatment.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:45.540
And so we have seen this
arc of how this has changed.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:48.780
And I would say, the last
three years in particular

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:52.600
has been sort of amazing,
but also challenging.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:58.690
So I would love to hear from
you, from your perspective,

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:01.830
how has the field changed,
and how we do this work?

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:06.220
And I'll start with Andrea, and
then Esteban, have you go second.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:06.970
ANDREA MADU: Sure.

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:10.420
So I'll caveat that I was working
in philanthropy during this time,

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:13.600
but I was working with a lot of
organizations that are addressing

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.700
the social determinants
of health and are

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.880
kind of working in
tandem with hospitals.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:20.080
I remember before the
pandemic, it was always

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:23.740
an uphill battle to try and convince
people why this work was important.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:27.280
We had the data, but the data
hadn't come into sharp focus yet.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.390
Once the pandemic hit, and it was
very clear that, one, leaders of color

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:34.420
were on the front lines and were
also having a higher rate of COVID,

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:38.290
and then the service providers as well
were from the same background, all

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:41.260
of a sudden, everyone was
investing in these organizations

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.468
led by people of color, who'd been doing
this work since the beginning-- were

00:07:44.468 --> 00:07:45.640
taking on an extra burden.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.980
And so it really did
bring into sharp focus

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:50.950
the idea that this pandemic is
something everyone's dealing with,

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:54.410
but it's actually affecting
these specific populations.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.340
So that was, to me, exciting to see,
because finally, the work that we

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:00.490
had been doing was very evident.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:04.150
But then, kind of a few years
later, and coming back to that work,

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.550
because this pandemic
has been declared over,

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:08.560
even though people are
still dealing with this,

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.140
I fear that there's kind
of a retracting now,

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:15.500
where that knowledge is kind of going
backwards and less focus is on it.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:19.482
So I think in my opinion, I
think we've moved forward,

00:08:19.482 --> 00:08:21.190
but then there's kind
of a pendulum swing

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:24.370
back, where I think more of that
work needs to continue to be done,

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:27.130
so that people are still educated
on why this is important.

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:29.005
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY:
Yeah, I think the burnout

00:08:29.005 --> 00:08:32.169
rate is high on a topic
like this, and it can be.

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:33.789
Esteban, what do you think?

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:37.370
ESTEBAN BARRETO: Yeah, and I agree
with the points that Andrea brought.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:41.096
And also, as you just said, a lot
has changed since the pandemic.

00:08:41.096 --> 00:08:43.179
I remember, right before
the pandemic, I was still

00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:45.040
working on my doctoral
dissertation, which

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:48.880
focused on the effects of
diversity, work culture,

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.770
on different organizational outcomes.

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:55.240
And I remember when I was trying
to argue for the need of having

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:57.680
a diverse work culture.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.320
It wasn't as clear.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:05.890
I felt there were a lot of pieces
that I was trying to really argue for.

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:10.990
But then, during the pandemic, as there
was an increased sense of urgency,

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:16.120
I feel like if I would have written
that dissertation during the pandemic

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:18.820
or after the pandemic, I
wouldn't have to explain myself

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:23.720
so much, in terms of the importance
of having a diverse workforce,

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:27.850
particularly in health
care settings, where

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:30.860
we serve diverse patient populations.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:34.810
So yes, I think it creates
a sense of urgency.

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:38.890
A lot of people wanted to know about it.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:44.890
Leaders focusing not only
their efforts, their resources,

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:48.850
but maybe reconsidering
their values at both

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:52.330
the professional and personal level.

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:56.890
But yet, there's a lot of
work that has to be done.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:10:02.620
I also agree with Andrea that
there may be some backtracking.

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:06.700
At the moment, I'm not sure
what that really is about,

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:10.000
but especially during the
first wave of the pandemic,

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:14.868
we did see a lot of energy in
trying to tackle these barriers.

00:10:14.868 --> 00:10:17.410
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: You know,
you raise an interesting point,

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:20.050
and Lenny, you and I have
talked about this-- this idea

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:22.270
that I think everybody
can now get on board

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:27.820
with the importance of diversifying
our workforce, our team, our providers.

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:30.490
And that seems a comfortable
space, to a certain extent,

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:33.280
for people to talk about,
particularly in health care.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:38.800
But this idea of, and it has to be
paired with equitable care, or making

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:41.740
sure that there's no racial
and ethnic disparities,

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:46.027
has sort of gotten less traction.

00:10:46.027 --> 00:10:47.860
Lenny, can you talk a
little bit about that,

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:51.490
from your perspective
of overseeing hospital

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:56.980
medicine in a clinical
setting, and being chief--

00:10:56.980 --> 00:10:57.985
the challenges of that?

00:11:00.407 --> 00:11:02.740
LENNY LOPEZ: Yeah, I'm
reflecting on what you're saying,

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:05.860
but also what Andrea and
Esteban were sharing.

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:09.340
Over 20 years ago, the
Institute of Medicine,

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:12.490
now known as the National Academies
of Medicine, Science, and Engineering,

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:14.320
put out a report on equal treatment.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:17.770
And 20 years ago, the
field was just beginning

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:21.610
the notion of collecting data on
race, ethnicity, and language.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.770
And at that point, we
had to justify that,

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:28.840
and we had to create reams of research
and papers and all of these things,

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:33.390
and policy, white papers, in order
to say this was an important issue.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.340
And I think the acceleration that's
happened in the last few years

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:39.550
has shifted not only the
public discourse, but also

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:43.510
the shift to structural
changes that are needed

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:46.120
within institutions and
between institutions

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:49.240
at both the government level, all the
way down to the health care system.

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:52.630
So the focus has become
less on individuals and more

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:58.120
on how the structures that we have
created as a health care system

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:01.820
perpetuate inequities, and how
can we change those things.

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:07.540
And so there is a level of comfort
that wasn't there even five years ago,

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:10.720
around, well, how do we
actually bring about the equity

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:16.210
that we all feel so important and it has
been front and center since the latest

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:20.950
social justice movements of George Floyd
and others who have suffered so much.

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:25.760
So I think the discussion really,
really has shifted in that direction.

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:29.200
So bringing that equity
back into the forefront,

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:33.350
into the center of the discussion--
that that's really where we're going.

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:37.930
And so data collection is very important
for monitoring and assuring quality,

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:41.140
but at the same time, we
need structural changes

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:44.960
that will allow for true
change over time to occur.

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:48.100
So I think that's really different
and very exciting about the work

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:51.420
that we have in front
of us moving forward.

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:53.850
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Yeah,
and it's interesting,

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:56.890
because I think there's a lot
more buy-in, but at the same time,

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:59.580
people are very overwhelmed
with what it would

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:05.460
take-- this idea of structural racism,
I feel, is fairly new to many folks.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:08.670
And I wonder, Lenny, or
even Andrea and Esteban,

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:12.240
if you feel that sometimes this
idea that it's structural racism

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:14.680
plays a role in it, and
it plays a very big role.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:19.470
And I don't know that we as a country
have really acknowledged, historically,

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:22.920
how things have been designed.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:27.300
But do you think that gives certain
people sort of the feeling of a pass?

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:34.200
Like, oh, it's not me, these things
that I can't change or control.

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:39.940
LENNY LOPEZ: Well, I would add that--

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:43.400
very briefly, and I'll let
Andrea respond as well--

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:46.120
is that there is an element
of that, and there's also

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:48.490
that sense of being
overwhelmed and realizing

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:51.790
how deep and large the
problem is, and that it's not

00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:54.010
a problem that just
started yesterday, but it's

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:58.100
been several years that have
allowed us to get to where we are.

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:00.730
So when people throw
their hands up in the air,

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:03.760
it could be the past
[INAUDIBLE] we all want

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:07.150
to feel that we're not
causing injury to others.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:10.990
But it's also the sense of, where
do I begin, and how did this start,

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:12.560
and what happened here.

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:15.460
So I think it's all of
that mixed together.

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:18.820
But I don't-- why don't I give Andrea
an opportunity to share her thoughts

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:19.885
as well.

00:14:19.885 --> 00:14:22.510
ANDREA MADU: I agree, Lenny, that
there's a sense of overwhelm.

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:25.690
I think people really resonate
with interpersonal racism.

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:28.630
Like, my additional
actions-- what can I do,

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:30.400
and what have I done that's caused harm?

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:32.740
But when you kind of zoom
out to the systemic lens,

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:35.980
and noticing that it's everywhere,
it's like you're a fish in water,

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:37.540
and it's all over you.

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:39.400
I think it is overwhelming for people.

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:41.080
But on the other side, I found that--

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.600
I've seen that a-ha moment
that happens with people

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:46.810
when they start to realize, oh,
this is like historic disinvestment,

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:49.360
and this has happened over
years, and it's all layered.

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:51.850
And I think for some people
who want to do that work,

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:54.730
that education is so important.

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:57.760
They can't-- people can't use overwhelm
as a reason not to do the work.

00:14:57.760 --> 00:14:59.635
I've seen the overwhelm
happen, but I've also

00:14:59.635 --> 00:15:02.800
seen that transformative, what can
I do, and how can we break down

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:04.540
some of the barriers institutionally.

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:07.390
And I think the course especially
works through that and talks

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:11.020
through the history of racism in the
US, and it kind of lays it in a way

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:14.410
that you really can't deny what's
going on, and just talks more

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:16.422
about tangible ways to address that.

00:15:16.422 --> 00:15:18.130
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY:
Yeah I feel like that

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:24.190
was a key piece for us to
include in our course was that we

00:15:24.190 --> 00:15:27.370
have to acknowledge how we got here.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:30.220
And it is not something
that is easy to talk about.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:35.480
But I think Dr. Powell does a wonderful
job of sort us walking us through.

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:37.120
And it can be very eye opening.

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:41.630
A lot of it is stuff that
we did not learn in school.

00:15:41.630 --> 00:15:44.020
And I think a lot of
people were surprised

00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:46.870
by some of the things he shared.

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:52.700
And so I think that's a key element that
we have sort of built into the course

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:54.350
that we need to start opening up.

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:55.133
Esteban.

00:15:55.133 --> 00:15:56.050
ESTEBAN BARRETO: Yeah.

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:59.950
I certainly agree with Lenny and Andrea
in terms of the overwhelming aspect

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:04.060
and also with Andrea's point about
how it shouldn't be an excuse.

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:07.690
Education is where we
should really focus.

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:10.150
Fortunately, we live in--

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.640
some of us live in
Massachusetts, where we're still

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:16.150
allowed to teach things
like critical race theory.

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:21.130
In terms of racism, I know there's
a lot of different definitions

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:23.500
and people may have different nuances.

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:27.100
I think of racism as a
system of oppression.

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:31.300
And then, from that, there's a
lot of different types of racism,

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:33.380
and racial discrimination, and all that.

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:37.540
And I think that that is a good
point, a good starting point,

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:44.110
the education of these concepts
and how this phenomena affects

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:46.280
different populations.

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:51.040
But again, I also agree with
the earlier point from Lenny

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:53.505
about the structural challenges.

00:16:53.505 --> 00:16:56.890
And we can do a whole lot
in terms of data collection

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:58.990
and trying to learn from
different perspectives.

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:03.220
But then if the structures and,
unfortunately, the politics

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:08.930
get in the way, it just makes
our job much, much harder.

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:12.342
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: You know,
it's a good point you make about--

00:17:12.342 --> 00:17:14.300
people sometimes ask me,
like, well, how do you

00:17:14.300 --> 00:17:16.520
know that equity has been achieved?

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:20.930
And I would say equity
is not the end goal.

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:22.849
Because it is really a journey.

00:17:22.849 --> 00:17:24.950
And it consistently has to be monitored.

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:26.825
It has to be deliberate.

00:17:26.825 --> 00:17:30.290
We are always learning something
new in this field, I feel.

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:31.910
It's constantly changing.

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:34.070
Terminology changes.

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:35.570
Funding changes.

00:17:35.570 --> 00:17:37.850
People's willingness to talk about it.

00:17:37.850 --> 00:17:40.460
We've definitely seen a huge change.

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:44.960
I mean, I think a couple years ago,
just mentioning the word racism

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:45.950
in a boardroom.

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:49.040
Like, everybody's shoulders
would be like this.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:50.690
And so now we're saying it.

00:17:50.690 --> 00:17:51.610
Yeah.

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:52.790
You remember?

00:17:52.790 --> 00:17:56.070
I mean, you try to do whatever you
can to bring the message along.

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:58.820
So you're like, OK,
let me just soften it.

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:03.092
And I'm happy to see that we can say
the word racism without stuttering,

00:18:03.092 --> 00:18:05.570
[LAUGHS] in boardrooms.

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:08.480
I mean, I feel like that's progress.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:11.090
And so this is really a journey.

00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:15.290
I'm curious to hear
from you three, what do

00:18:15.290 --> 00:18:17.210
you think we really need in the field?

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:18.590
Like, what is the gap?

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:23.390
There's a lot here, interpersonal
racism, structural racism.

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:26.210
If we think about health care
organizations and health care

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:28.970
in general, what is needed,
from your perspective?

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:34.950
LENNY LOPEZ: One thing I
wanted to add to that--

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:39.120
to answer your question, to add to the
conversation, is that what is needed

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:41.970
IS not only I love
this frame of journey,

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:45.670
also just continued growth
as part of that journey.

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:48.690
I am happy to share that
even after 20 years working

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:53.650
in the field of equity and health care
disparities, I'm constantly learning.

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:55.650
And I'm constantly being pushed.

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:59.490
And I'm constantly needing to
reflect on my own implicit biases,

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:04.020
and my own starting
point, and my own ability

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:06.570
to communicate these things
in the right settings

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:09.100
with the different groups of people.

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:11.670
And I think those are things
that, of course like this,

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:15.600
can really give you that space, no
matter what your level of experience,

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:19.590
or background, or education
around these topics.

00:19:19.590 --> 00:19:22.780
it is really, as you're
saying, is a personal journey.

00:19:22.780 --> 00:19:25.800
So I think one of the things
that when this course comes

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:29.460
live-- and I'll encourage other folks
to take it-- is, really, to realize

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:31.320
that you don't know everything.

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:33.540
And there's so much space
where you can actually

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:36.360
continue to grow as
an individual and make

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:40.800
those changes in your own place
in the universe where you are,

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:42.878
at work and in the communities.

00:19:42.878 --> 00:19:43.920
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Yeah.

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:47.460
And I will say, like, we had
long discussions about content

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:50.160
when we started designing this course.

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:52.530
And there's probably
stuff that we weren't

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:57.480
able to include because,
honestly, to include everything,

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:59.715
your course would be a full year.

00:19:59.715 --> 00:20:01.840
And then you would still
probably leave things out.

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:03.660
So I think we really
tried to focus on what

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:06.660
we know best from all the
organizations that we've worked with.

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:10.950
What we're the core competencies
that we would want you to know?

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:12.840
What would be some of
the building blocks

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:18.870
that you, as a person, having to think
about transforming your organization

00:20:18.870 --> 00:20:23.340
to think about racial and ethnic
disparities, to think about equity?

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:26.702
What are some of the key components
and really bringing in the folks

00:20:26.702 --> 00:20:27.660
that we've worked with?

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:31.320
So I totally agree that--

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:32.430
with your point, Lenny.

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:34.317
Andrea and Esteban.

00:20:34.317 --> 00:20:35.900
ANDREW MADU: Yeah, I agree too, Lenny.

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:37.620
That humility is so important.

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:41.540
And I think humility was something I
felt throughout this course, the idea

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:45.260
that equity is not something that
we're going to solve in our lifetime.

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:49.400
But there are pieces of it that
we can move along a journey

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:50.640
while we're working on it.

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:53.480
And so I think that was
very helpful to see.

00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:56.000
I think, also, just in
general, structurally,

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:58.700
I think what's needed
is a pipeline of doctors

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:01.670
who also looks like the communities
that they're serving and have

00:21:01.670 --> 00:21:06.020
that lived experience to understand
the cultural context of what patients

00:21:06.020 --> 00:21:06.650
are saying.

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:07.490
That's one.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:10.550
And then I also think this understanding
of the medical mistrust that

00:21:10.550 --> 00:21:15.200
has happened over decades or centuries
and understanding why some people might

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:17.450
not want to come to the
doctor or why people might not

00:21:17.450 --> 00:21:18.650
be adhering to treatments.

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:20.160
There are reasons behind that.

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:23.420
And I think really coming to it
with both humility and empathy

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:25.082
to patients I think is so important.

00:21:25.082 --> 00:21:27.290
And that kind of goes into
the frame of understanding

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:29.535
the history behind everything.

00:21:29.535 --> 00:21:30.410
ESTEBAN BARRETO: Yes.

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:35.510
And I definitely agree with what
was said, definitely a journey.

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:38.330
We have to exercise humility.

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:40.490
Andrea, your comment had
me thinking about how

00:21:40.490 --> 00:21:43.340
we move from cultural
competence to cultural humility.

00:21:43.340 --> 00:21:46.776
So maybe we have to think
about equity, humility.

00:21:46.776 --> 00:21:52.970
And it is not easy to
address health disparities

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:55.460
when there are also
disparities in education,

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:56.990
disparities in the legal system.

00:21:56.990 --> 00:22:02.360
And everything kind of has
an effect on each other.

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:05.870
So in order to increase representation
among health professionals,

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:09.410
you also have to address
disparities and equal opportunity

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:13.400
to education, what
children and young adults

00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:16.230
are being exposed to, in
terms of future career.

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:20.030
So I think, as in society, there's
a lot that we still need to work on.

00:22:22.670 --> 00:22:26.520
We're talking more in particular
about health care disparities.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:32.240
But I just wanted to make that point
that disparities are really everywhere

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:34.100
in every single system.

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:42.140
So we also have to think about tackling
in other fabrics of our society.

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:47.210
One thing about this course is that,
considering how complex this issue is,

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:55.050
the speakers do explain the topics
in a very comprehensive manner

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:58.310
and provide an examples,
providing different case

00:22:58.310 --> 00:23:01.800
scenarios that I was really happy with.

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:05.120
And I found it very
easy to follow along.

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:08.790
And I also have to admit that
I did learn a lot of things,

00:23:08.790 --> 00:23:11.968
despite having a few years
working on this field.

00:23:11.968 --> 00:23:13.010
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Yeah.

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:19.340
Andrea and Esteban were the
early adopters of the course.

00:23:19.340 --> 00:23:21.752
They actually went through the course.

00:23:21.752 --> 00:23:27.810
And I just want to thank you, first of
all, for taking the time to do that.

00:23:27.810 --> 00:23:30.110
But it's very meaningful
to me that somebody

00:23:30.110 --> 00:23:36.480
like you gave it a seal of approval and
that you still learned something new.

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:42.380
I think that's an important theme that
Lenny and all of you have brought up

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:46.250
is that when people talk about
subject matter experts, I say,

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:47.810
oh, that's with a caveat.

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:52.670
Because, to be honest, I'm
constantly learning new things.

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:57.200
I'm constantly-- in this work,
you have to self-reflect.

00:23:57.200 --> 00:24:00.740
That's really the only area where
you can make some concrete changes.

00:24:00.740 --> 00:24:04.490
But you have to reflect on your
own journey, your own reaction

00:24:04.490 --> 00:24:07.730
to some of what you work on every day.

00:24:07.730 --> 00:24:10.430
And then also, it just changes.

00:24:10.430 --> 00:24:11.570
It changes constantly.

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:15.890
It depends who's in leadership
positions, whether it's in this country

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:17.180
or in your institution.

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:20.430
It depends on where the money's going.

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:23.520
So there's a lot of factors.

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:25.010
And so I agree.

00:24:25.010 --> 00:24:28.460
I feel like if you're not
learning anything new,

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:32.630
then maybe, I don't know
that you're ready because I

00:24:32.630 --> 00:24:35.840
feel like we're constantly learning.

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:44.232
We are sort of early adopters,
probably because [LAUGHS] we've

00:24:44.232 --> 00:24:45.440
been doing this work so long.

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:50.600
And I just-- I'm just kind of
curious, from your perspective,

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:53.940
in just giving some--
like, on hindsight,

00:24:53.940 --> 00:24:56.690
I wish I had known this
when doing this work.

00:24:56.690 --> 00:25:01.980
Like, what are some things that
you would share with the audience?

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:04.720
From whatever role you
feel is most comfortable,

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:07.950
whether it's, like, a physician
leadership role, where it's, Esteban,

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:13.080
as evaluation component
or, Andrea, as, like,

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:20.020
a consultant with a really heavy
vested interest in this topic,

00:25:20.020 --> 00:25:23.380
or from a personal perspective.

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:26.080
ESTEBAN BARRETO: So I can talk
about the evaluation work.

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:31.650
One thing that I wish I knew before is
that we love timelines, and deadlines,

00:25:31.650 --> 00:25:32.400
and all of that.

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:36.750
In an ideal world, we are
able to meet all the deadlines

00:25:36.750 --> 00:25:38.160
for all of our projects.

00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:41.430
But then the projects that
have more of an equity lens,

00:25:41.430 --> 00:25:45.600
of a health disparities lens, I feel
like you need to be a little bit more

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:48.570
flexible and sometimes pivot.

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:53.800
So when you may plan the perfect
goal, the perfect objective,

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:56.790
you may come up with a perfect
approach to data collection.

00:25:56.790 --> 00:26:01.755
And then you may learn that there's
things that you may need to change.

00:26:01.755 --> 00:26:08.950
And so I wish that that was one
component that I learned early on.

00:26:08.950 --> 00:26:11.310
But now, that is
something that I do stress

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:13.980
when I do meet with
different investigators who

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:18.180
are interested in having an equity
lens to their research portfolio.

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:23.190
And we talk about that flexibility
or that ability to pivot when needed.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:28.470
ANDREA MADU: I think one
thing I wish I had known

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:33.210
is, one, like, how to identify people
who their minds are just never going

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:35.342
to be changed, no matter what you do.

00:26:35.342 --> 00:26:37.050
But I wish I had
learned those strategies

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:40.140
to kind of transform the hearts
and minds around those people.

00:26:40.140 --> 00:26:44.010
Because, really, that's how it works
to get those allies on your side.

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:47.580
I think there's one piece of the course
that talks about the Kotter model.

00:26:47.580 --> 00:26:50.392
And I think I've always thought
of creating a sense of urgency

00:26:50.392 --> 00:26:51.600
was the most important piece.

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.420
But there's all of these pieces
of creating the coalition,

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:56.430
but then also try to use
that coalition to get

00:26:56.430 --> 00:26:58.510
other people involved and invested.

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:02.040
And that's really what pushes these
initiatives to be successful, more so

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:04.330
than just saying, this
is why this is important.

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:06.045
So I wish I had known that before.

00:27:06.045 --> 00:27:07.920
I think now it's really
important in the work

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:10.200
that I do now and especially
in this health care work,

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:12.150
where you are going to have
to change hearts and minds.

00:27:12.150 --> 00:27:14.010
And how do you do that
when you might not

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:15.635
be the right messenger for the message?

00:27:15.635 --> 00:27:18.010
But you could find that person
to help you along the way.

00:27:18.010 --> 00:27:19.052
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Yeah.

00:27:19.052 --> 00:27:19.920
It was very helpful.

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:22.260
The Kotter model isn't
designed for equity.

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:27.010
But when I read his book, I was
like, oh, my God, this is it.

00:27:27.010 --> 00:27:33.390
This is the template of why it's so hard
to change an organization, like, really

00:27:33.390 --> 00:27:36.180
all the way through.

00:27:36.180 --> 00:27:40.140
And it's-- when you talk about
organizational transformation,

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:44.110
he's identified what
makes it successful.

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:48.453
So I think that's a very helpful
grounding in, sort of, this work, when

00:27:48.453 --> 00:27:50.370
you kind of feel like,
oh, I don't know, like,

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:52.662
why is it that I've been
doing this work for five years

00:27:52.662 --> 00:27:55.440
but it feels like I've got no traction?

00:27:55.440 --> 00:28:00.030
Then I think that model is, from a
business perspective, very helpful.

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:01.170
Lenny?

00:28:01.170 --> 00:28:02.100
LENNY LOPEZ: Yeah.

00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:04.470
Reflecting on what
everyone is sharing, one

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:07.860
of the things that I've learned over
time in working with organizations,

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:13.110
working within my own organization,
to address the systemic barriers

00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:19.200
is that all the sources of disparities
and the solutions for eliminating

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:24.130
those disparities are all
hyperlocal and that you can learn--

00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:27.630
The goal is to learn tools and
strategies, for example, the Kotter

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:30.780
model we're talking
about, to really, then,

00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:34.930
take it home, and take it to your
institution, to your organization,

00:28:34.930 --> 00:28:38.500
to your community, and
your place in the universe.

00:28:38.500 --> 00:28:41.080
And that's where the change happens.

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:46.950
And so although we have oftentimes
grandiose ideas about how the world is

00:28:46.950 --> 00:28:50.010
going to change and all of
these things, oftentimes, it's

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:53.470
much smaller victories
that add up over time.

00:28:53.470 --> 00:28:55.800
And so those are the
things where we're, Aswita,

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:58.770
to use your metaphor,
the journey, those are

00:28:58.770 --> 00:29:03.210
the things that give you strength,
and courage, and the energy

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:04.810
to keep doing the work.

00:29:04.810 --> 00:29:09.030
And so reframing and organizing your
thinking in that way, essentially

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:12.990
what's available in this course,
is an incredibly helpful process

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:15.560
to go through.

00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:19.450
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: So my last
question to all three of you is,

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:21.400
this is hard work.

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:25.540
I do feel like once you see
things, you can't unsee them.

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:27.100
And then you see them everywhere.

00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:32.580
Like, if you go on vacation,
[LAUGHS] in the grocery store.

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:38.140
And so my question to you is how
do you do self-care in this work?

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:42.050
Like, I love that all four of
us have the lived experience.

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:46.150
It allows me not to have to do the
preamble of why this is important.

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:49.430
I can just skip that and go
straight to how can we do things.

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:51.535
But I really would
love to hear from you,

00:29:51.535 --> 00:29:53.920
what is the self-care strategy you use.

00:29:53.920 --> 00:30:00.340
And mine, for example, is I don't read,
outside of this job, any heavy books

00:30:00.340 --> 00:30:03.910
on racism, social justice.

00:30:03.910 --> 00:30:05.590
I don't watch movies about it.

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:07.390
I just-- I've had enough.

00:30:07.390 --> 00:30:13.270
i watch really bad trash TV
and read detective novels.

00:30:13.270 --> 00:30:17.710
[LAUGHS] But I just know that
I need to refill my tank.

00:30:17.710 --> 00:30:22.270
So I would love to hear from the three
of you your strategy for doing that.

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:25.900
Because I think that's an
important piece of all of this work

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:27.140
is the self-care.

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:29.800
So I'll start with
Esteban, and then Andrea,

00:30:29.800 --> 00:30:31.433
and then I'll close it out with Lenny.

00:30:31.433 --> 00:30:32.350
ESTEBAN BARRETO: Yeah.

00:30:32.350 --> 00:30:37.480
For me, some strategies for
self-care is family and friends

00:30:37.480 --> 00:30:41.920
having open discussions
about different issues.

00:30:41.920 --> 00:30:46.420
I do have to admit that I try my
best to stay current to the news

00:30:46.420 --> 00:30:49.600
and everything that is going on.

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:55.480
And I learn how to not take
things personally, over the years.

00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:59.170
Also, another big part for
me is working with students.

00:30:59.170 --> 00:31:02.050
I'm an instructor at the
School of Social Work Simmons.

00:31:02.050 --> 00:31:04.975
And I teach a class each semester.

00:31:04.975 --> 00:31:07.180
And working with different
students really allowed

00:31:07.180 --> 00:31:10.270
me to also stay current in
different topics, for example,

00:31:10.270 --> 00:31:13.810
social issues, the different
nuances, the different ways

00:31:13.810 --> 00:31:17.260
that issues are being looked at.

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:21.820
And so those are some of the things
that do help me to stay current,

00:31:21.820 --> 00:31:24.342
but also trying to
practice of self-care.

00:31:24.342 --> 00:31:26.050
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY:
Yeah, there's nothing

00:31:26.050 --> 00:31:33.040
like working with young students to get
out, the old, archaic way of thinking.

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:34.390
And it's sort of refreshing.

00:31:34.390 --> 00:31:35.110
Andrea?

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:35.860
ANDREA MADU: Yeah.

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:37.930
I feel like I plug in a lot.

00:31:37.930 --> 00:31:40.662
Because of my work, I really
have to stay current as well.

00:31:40.662 --> 00:31:42.370
I have a group chat
with my family, where

00:31:42.370 --> 00:31:45.040
I'm just like, anytime something
crazy happens, I'm just like,

00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:45.970
can you believe this?

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:47.262
And we just kind of dish on it.

00:31:47.262 --> 00:31:49.810
And it makes me feel like I'm not alone.

00:31:49.810 --> 00:31:53.200
I also schedule 30 minutes before
I have a big meeting with someone

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:56.320
that I know is going to be, like,
a struggle, to go on a walk,

00:31:56.320 --> 00:31:58.090
or I listen to some
of my favorite music,

00:31:58.090 --> 00:32:00.970
just to get me kind of
hyped up to deal with that.

00:32:00.970 --> 00:32:04.270
But, yeah, just creating space for joy,
I think it's so important to find joy

00:32:04.270 --> 00:32:06.640
wherever you can in this work
because I know it's tough.

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:08.290
But those moments do exist.

00:32:08.290 --> 00:32:11.603
And I really enjoy working with people
who are just as passionate about it.

00:32:11.603 --> 00:32:12.520
ESTEBAN BARRETO: Yeah.

00:32:12.520 --> 00:32:13.978
That's a good point about the chat.

00:32:13.978 --> 00:32:17.740
I think we all have folks that we
text to where it's like, oh my God,

00:32:17.740 --> 00:32:19.060
this person just said this.

00:32:19.060 --> 00:32:20.380
Can you believe it?

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:23.830
And then they'll ping back and,
like, I can't, and I'm really sorry.

00:32:23.830 --> 00:32:25.790
And that's really all
sometimes you need.

00:32:25.790 --> 00:32:26.290
Lenny?

00:32:28.790 --> 00:32:29.540
LENNY LOPEZ: Yeah.

00:32:29.540 --> 00:32:34.580
Similarly to all of
the panelists, I'm just

00:32:34.580 --> 00:32:37.340
thrilled that we're even having
the conversation about self-care

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:38.000
in this space.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:41.990
Because I think even 10
years ago, we weren't

00:32:41.990 --> 00:32:47.510
talking about self-care and for those of
us doing equity work and justice work,

00:32:47.510 --> 00:32:49.700
and realizing that you
have to give yourself

00:32:49.700 --> 00:32:53.410
that space in that condition to realize
that the work is larger than yourself.

00:32:53.410 --> 00:32:57.530
And so the time that you put into
it, the education, the courses,

00:32:57.530 --> 00:32:59.880
the things you do, the
work we do every day,

00:32:59.880 --> 00:33:05.480
those are all part of a larger change,
a larger process that you're a part of

00:33:05.480 --> 00:33:06.950
and you're adding to.

00:33:06.950 --> 00:33:08.430
But it is not all you.

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:11.030
And I think that is really liberating.

00:33:11.030 --> 00:33:13.970
But I'm so thrilled that we
talk about self-care these days

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:18.230
because it's so essential
to not be the hero that

00:33:18.230 --> 00:33:24.240
dies because of the weight of
inequity and injustice in the world,

00:33:24.240 --> 00:33:26.230
so very, very, very important.

00:33:26.230 --> 00:33:27.230
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Yes.

00:33:27.230 --> 00:33:31.310
I mean, if we think about reducing the
stress so that we have better health

00:33:31.310 --> 00:33:35.240
outcome, self-care is a big component.

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:39.670
Because the data shows [CHUCKLES]
we're not winning in this spot at all.

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:40.670
ANDREA MADU: Not at all.

00:33:40.670 --> 00:33:41.990
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:44.540
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY:
OK, I think we're going

00:33:44.540 --> 00:33:46.980
to turn to some audience questions.

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:49.012
Thank you for that.

00:33:49.012 --> 00:33:50.720
I think that's an
important piece that we

00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:53.210
didn't sort of talk
about in the course but I

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:55.590
wanted to highlight in the webinar.

00:33:55.590 --> 00:33:58.760
So I think there's been
a lot of questions.

00:33:58.760 --> 00:34:07.130
And I'm-- OK, first question, can
you outline some of the tangible ways

00:34:07.130 --> 00:34:10.400
to address racial inequities
in health care. what tactical,

00:34:10.400 --> 00:34:13.850
practical steps can be taken now.

00:34:13.850 --> 00:34:15.484
Aside from taking the course--

00:34:15.484 --> 00:34:19.400
[LAUGHS] which outline [INAUDIBLE]?

00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:23.119
Maybe, let's see, anybody
want to take that question on?

00:34:28.183 --> 00:34:30.100
LENNY LOPEZ: Well, it's
a very large question.

00:34:30.100 --> 00:34:35.440
And it comes back to my comment
about the sources of disparities,

00:34:35.440 --> 00:34:37.870
the solutions of disparities
being hyperlocal.

00:34:37.870 --> 00:34:44.020
So, for example, for me, as a clinical
leader at a very large university, what

00:34:44.020 --> 00:34:48.429
we've been doing for many years
is starting from the process,

00:34:48.429 --> 00:34:53.679
before people engage our health system,
the whole process of registration,

00:34:53.679 --> 00:34:55.570
the process of having
access to health care,

00:34:55.570 --> 00:34:59.500
and actually structuring it to
prevent the blind spots, that

00:34:59.500 --> 00:35:02.050
prevent the processes that
will lead to inequity,

00:35:02.050 --> 00:35:06.220
and then building at every level,
from that moment of registration,

00:35:06.220 --> 00:35:09.220
to the clinical care, to the
training of the providers,

00:35:09.220 --> 00:35:13.240
to the measurement of outcomes, to
the reporting, public reporting,

00:35:13.240 --> 00:35:18.220
of those outcomes, on and on at each
step of the way, may sound not sexy.

00:35:18.220 --> 00:35:20.440
But that's actually how
change happens and how equity

00:35:20.440 --> 00:35:23.230
is established in an institution--

00:35:23.230 --> 00:35:24.560
institutional setting.

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:27.490
So that would be the
beginning of a conversation

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:30.730
about the answer to that
question, that this is actually

00:35:30.730 --> 00:35:35.770
deep work that requires every level
of the institution to be involved in.

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:38.230
ANDREA MADU: I think I would
add to that, to treat patients

00:35:38.230 --> 00:35:40.240
as the subject matter
experts of their lives.

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:43.240
Because there's so much information
that can be gleaned just

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:45.340
by having that rapport with a patient.

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:48.640
And I think in designing solutions,
like Lenny's saying, they're hyperlocal,

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:51.520
I think that also means just having
people within the community that

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:55.570
are trusted to understand where the core
issues are, understand the root causes,

00:35:55.570 --> 00:35:58.340
before we kind of go into
the treatment aspect of it.

00:35:58.340 --> 00:36:01.480
So I think bringing in the community
in and building that trust as well.

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:03.730
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Esteban,
did you have any comment?

00:36:03.730 --> 00:36:04.647
ESTEBAN BARRETO: Yeah.

00:36:04.647 --> 00:36:07.300
I agree with Lenny and Andrea.

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:12.670
The only thing I will add is
we all know that if you're

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:16.990
trying to find disparities and
you look for them, will find them.

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:20.980
And after you take the course, and
while you're taking the course,

00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:23.110
and you're thinking about
a particular project

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:25.300
that you would like to
embark on, I'll suggest

00:36:25.300 --> 00:36:30.235
that you get as specific
as you can in your project.

00:36:30.235 --> 00:36:32.110
One of the things that
I see for my students,

00:36:32.110 --> 00:36:35.980
for example, is that their
goals are very broad.

00:36:35.980 --> 00:36:39.820
And we're all trying to solve
everything in the world.

00:36:39.820 --> 00:36:42.800
And, unfortunately, we may
not have the bandwidth.

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:48.040
So I will say, at the individual
project level, the more specific

00:36:48.040 --> 00:36:54.970
you get, the more tailored, the better--

00:36:54.970 --> 00:37:00.610
the most successful you will be to
measure and keep track of the progress.

00:37:00.610 --> 00:37:03.430
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Yeah, try
not to boil the ocean, I think.

00:37:03.430 --> 00:37:07.150
The other piece, sort of
bouncing back to what Lenny said,

00:37:07.150 --> 00:37:12.010
is I do think data collection,
that is a tangible step that you

00:37:12.010 --> 00:37:16.330
can take as to assess the
quality of your demographic data.

00:37:16.330 --> 00:37:18.580
Because out of that comes a lot.

00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:23.320
And then, I would say, taking a journey
and looking around in your community

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:27.250
and seeing what are some of
the historical milestones,

00:37:27.250 --> 00:37:32.800
things, events that happened that
created the mistrust in the communities

00:37:32.800 --> 00:37:35.920
that you serve between the
communities and your organization?

00:37:35.920 --> 00:37:39.100
We don't like to do
that, but we have to.

00:37:39.100 --> 00:37:44.260
I think that's core to understanding
whether our interventions

00:37:44.260 --> 00:37:46.400
or our strategies are going to work.

00:37:46.400 --> 00:37:50.650
They won't work if we don't acknowledge
some of the historical events that

00:37:50.650 --> 00:37:52.720
have created a lot of the mistrust.

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:54.703
We have to do that hard work.

00:37:54.703 --> 00:37:57.620
ANDREA MADU: I just want to quickly
add to what you're saying, Aswita,

00:37:57.620 --> 00:38:01.340
and what Andrea was saying, is that,
in this course and in this work,

00:38:01.340 --> 00:38:05.130
you have to realize that you're
creating partnerships and ally shifts.

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:09.905
You're not going into a
community to save a community.

00:38:09.905 --> 00:38:12.710
Those approaches act
as if the communities

00:38:12.710 --> 00:38:16.550
have no resilience, no empowerment,
no resources, et cetera,

00:38:16.550 --> 00:38:18.810
when it's often not the case.

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:23.240
So it's really that connection where
the community, and the systems,

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:27.980
and the institutions work together
that leads to long-lasting change.

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:30.230
Because institutions
are within communities,

00:38:30.230 --> 00:38:32.750
whether they want to
think about that or not.

00:38:32.750 --> 00:38:34.430
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Yeah.

00:38:34.430 --> 00:38:35.910
This is a good question.

00:38:35.910 --> 00:38:38.870
I get this a lot when
we do presentations for,

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:43.190
like, an international audience, which
is, do you feel like racial disparities

00:38:43.190 --> 00:38:47.150
and discrimination is a bigger
problem in the United States

00:38:47.150 --> 00:38:48.770
and in health care in particular?

00:38:48.770 --> 00:38:51.530
Or do you see similar
issues internationally?

00:38:54.850 --> 00:38:58.020
ESTEBAN BARRETO: So I could start by
saying that something to keep in mind

00:38:58.020 --> 00:39:04.920
is that the way that we conceptualize
these concepts differ in each country.

00:39:04.920 --> 00:39:07.620
Even the concept of
race is very different,

00:39:07.620 --> 00:39:10.860
the way that we think about
it here in the US versus it

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:14.730
started in Latin America,
in Europe, et cetera.

00:39:14.730 --> 00:39:18.390
So that's something that
I wish to say, to keep

00:39:18.390 --> 00:39:22.820
in mind that a lot of these
constructs, a lot of these concepts

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:28.755
are particular to a particular culture,
country, and also, moment in time.

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:34.170
ANDREA MADU: I would also
say, again, would echo

00:39:34.170 --> 00:39:36.870
that, that racism is seen differently.

00:39:36.870 --> 00:39:38.940
Racism's twin sister is colonialism.

00:39:38.940 --> 00:39:42.300
And there's colonialism all over the
world that has affected populations.

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:44.610
I think any community
where you see there's

00:39:44.610 --> 00:39:47.535
a gradation in color, the people
who are the darkest skinned

00:39:47.535 --> 00:39:50.160
are generally the ones that are
facing the most discrimination.

00:39:50.160 --> 00:39:52.013
And you will find
disparities there as well.

00:39:52.013 --> 00:39:54.180
So I think just because the
conception is different,

00:39:54.180 --> 00:39:57.460
it doesn't mean that those
disparities don't exist.

00:39:57.460 --> 00:39:59.910
It's just that I think in the
US, we talk about it more.

00:39:59.910 --> 00:40:04.210
And it's, like, we have this history of
slavery as well for hundreds of years.

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:07.118
So I think it's just a difference
of familiarity with the concept.

00:40:07.118 --> 00:40:08.160
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Yeah.

00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:11.040
And I would say my home
country is the Netherlands.

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:13.290
And it's known as a
very tolerant country.

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:16.680
But even if we look
deeper under the hood,

00:40:16.680 --> 00:40:20.250
there's immigrant
populations who are not

00:40:20.250 --> 00:40:26.190
getting the same treatment as
somebody who is Dutch and born there.

00:40:26.190 --> 00:40:32.310
There's also religious oppression
that probably mirrors the same thing.

00:40:32.310 --> 00:40:34.380
And then there's tribal as well.

00:40:34.380 --> 00:40:36.374
We have tribal differences.

00:40:36.374 --> 00:40:44.570
So I would say it's really about what
kind of social justice and oppression

00:40:44.570 --> 00:40:46.600
do you need to look at,
rather than thinking

00:40:46.600 --> 00:40:51.010
that racial and ethnic
disparities is just a US problem.

00:40:51.010 --> 00:40:56.950
That's sort of denying that your
country may also have issues.

00:40:56.950 --> 00:40:57.460
Lenny?

00:40:57.460 --> 00:40:58.210
LENNY LOPEZ: Yeah.

00:40:58.210 --> 00:41:00.340
And I would add to what
everyone is saying.

00:41:00.340 --> 00:41:02.380
And I think it's also a false dichotomy.

00:41:02.380 --> 00:41:08.020
Because in reality, whoever raises the
discussion point, or socioeconomics

00:41:08.020 --> 00:41:13.810
is the social point, or colonialism
history is the point, all of them

00:41:13.810 --> 00:41:16.460
become deeply interrelated over time.

00:41:16.460 --> 00:41:21.670
So when you try to separate one out,
the other is still embedded in it.

00:41:21.670 --> 00:41:26.050
And so I tend to tell people
that whether the terms that

00:41:26.050 --> 00:41:30.160
are more primary in our context
are meaningful to people,

00:41:30.160 --> 00:41:34.430
realizing that they're actually very
similar and they're very correlated.

00:41:34.430 --> 00:41:37.540
So trying to separate
those out often leads you

00:41:37.540 --> 00:41:43.242
to confusing conclusions
that are usually not true.

00:41:43.242 --> 00:41:44.200
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: OK.

00:41:44.200 --> 00:41:49.450
One more question and
then we'll close it out.

00:41:49.450 --> 00:41:55.130
And, Lenny, maybe I'll punt this to you.

00:41:55.130 --> 00:41:58.270
So how can we integrate
training on reducing disparities

00:41:58.270 --> 00:41:59.900
in formal medical training?

00:41:59.900 --> 00:42:01.870
How can medical students
carry this workout?

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:04.360
Medical students are
the next generation.

00:42:04.360 --> 00:42:10.060
And so we have to be really mindful
of being able to focus on them

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:12.170
because that is a wave
of change, as we see it.

00:42:12.170 --> 00:42:14.663
So any thoughts on that question?

00:42:14.663 --> 00:42:15.830
LENNY LOPEZ: Great question.

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:20.370
I think about this a lot, given that I
have trainees always working with me.

00:42:20.370 --> 00:42:23.210
And that is is that
the only way to do it

00:42:23.210 --> 00:42:27.300
is that it has to be actually embedded
in the four years of a medical school

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:31.910
curriculum, from the very
beginning, and not only the content,

00:42:31.910 --> 00:42:36.710
but the making the space,
formal space, for reflection,

00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:41.090
or mentoring, or observation of what
other senior doctors may be doing,

00:42:41.090 --> 00:42:44.220
handling these kinds of issues
in the clinical context.

00:42:44.220 --> 00:42:47.510
So it actually has to
be part of a culture.

00:42:47.510 --> 00:42:51.140
And that is done through the
investment of money and time,

00:42:51.140 --> 00:42:54.530
protecting educators
times to actually be

00:42:54.530 --> 00:42:57.770
able to teach this material,
as opposed to volunteerism,

00:42:57.770 --> 00:42:59.420
really embedding it in.

00:42:59.420 --> 00:43:01.670
This is an essential
topic that every year,

00:43:01.670 --> 00:43:04.620
we're going to discuss
throughout the course work.

00:43:04.620 --> 00:43:07.130
But again, that takes
time, money, and investment

00:43:07.130 --> 00:43:09.030
from the leadership of the institution.

00:43:09.030 --> 00:43:13.650
So very, very important if we're
going to really change the needle.

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:20.420
But it can't be a niche topic that's
done in a small, 10-hour seminar once

00:43:20.420 --> 00:43:22.130
or twice in four years.

00:43:22.130 --> 00:43:24.080
That is not going to work.

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:27.530
ASWITA TAN-MCGRORY: Yeah,
multipronged effort, definitely.

00:43:27.530 --> 00:43:33.650
I want to thank the three of you
for joining me in this conversation.

00:43:33.650 --> 00:43:36.650
I knew that we would
have a good conversation.

00:43:36.650 --> 00:43:40.760
I stacked the deck by asking highly
intelligent friends and colleagues

00:43:40.760 --> 00:43:44.340
to join me on the panel
who I really like.

00:43:44.340 --> 00:43:47.060
That just is very helpful and makes it--

00:43:47.060 --> 00:43:51.140
I knew all of you would show up and that
you would bring your authentic self,

00:43:51.140 --> 00:43:54.990
and your lived experience, and your
thoughtfulness to this webinar.

00:43:54.990 --> 00:43:56.990
So thank you again.

00:43:56.990 --> 00:44:01.890
And I'm now going to turn it
over to Jim to close us out.

00:44:01.890 --> 00:44:02.390
Great.

00:44:02.390 --> 00:44:03.440
Thank you, Aswita.

00:44:03.440 --> 00:44:05.600
And thank you, Lenny,
Esteban, and Andrea.

00:44:05.600 --> 00:44:08.340
And thanks to all of you
who've joined us today.

00:44:08.340 --> 00:44:11.300
We hope you'll continue the
